reports.summary:
I brought the five test files of my group in line with rework 2 without weakening them, and added tests for the new behaviour. All 976 tests pass. They also pass run file by file, all together, and in reverse file order with -p no:randomly. ruff check and ruff format --check pass on all five files.

What I changed in each file:
- test_model_version.py: the ACTIVE activation fixture now states evaluation_data_origin. There is a new MDL-7 case for a missing evaluation origin. The 'validated' tests now require a non-fixture training AND evaluation origin; this covers fixture training with real evaluation, real training with fixture evaluation, a rule set validated by its evaluation alone, and only ACTIVE ever being validated. The registry tests reflect that components must be registered before the composite: the refusal now names the composite's first entry. There are new direct RegistryHistory tests: check() records nothing, admit() records, model(), active() across activation, retirement and rollback, one ACTIVE version per (kind, model_name), and a refused entry changes nothing. The rollback must name the same-family version that was active immediately before: an earlier rolled-back version, another family's version or an unregistered one is refused. Wrong argument types must raise a GieError.
- test_run.py: rewritten for content-derived run ids. Changing any single field changes derive_run_id, and every non-run_id field is checked. The RUN-1 cases cover a mismatched run_id after changing the configuration, label, seed, the dirty-tree flag or the models. RUN-2 adds the creation-time rules, and there are new RUN-5 accepted and refused cases. Violation cases re-derive run_id so that RUN-2..5 are not hidden behind RUN-1, which is checked first. Also covered: RunManifest.create, derive_run_id refusing an unknown, missing or malformed field and a live mode, RunModel and RMD-1 (constructed and decoded), covers() at its boundaries, model(id, time_ns) over a walk-forward schedule, the MAX_RUN_MODELS bound, risk_regime, code_tree_dirty, the removal of model_version_ids, and the new ordering time (data_start_ns for a replay, created_time_ns for paper). RunContext now hands out ClockView and SchedulerView, and the tests check that a component cannot advance, begin a step, poll or fire timers through them. Every missing or foreign argument, the sink included, is a ConfigError. seed_for matches derive_seed and differs per purpose, and is refused with a ConfigError when the run has no seed. Paper runs use PaperClock with PollingScheduler.
- test_journal.py: the shared run fixture is now samples.run_story() plus the model registry history and a clock reading (32 records), with named line constants that a test of their own checks. Old tests were rewritten to start from valid prefixes of the run, because the ledger is now referential and a derived record needs an input first. New tests cover:
  - the order of the checks: run, then stream position, then provenance, then time, then ledger;
  - provenance against the manifest: record data origin, a run origin better than a tick's, an undeclared tick source, the replay-period boundaries (and no period in a paper run), an unpinned model, a status that differs from the pinned one, a model used outside its pinned period, and sizing quality from a model that is not pinned as validated;
  - the time rules: with a ReplayClock or PaperClock the ordering time must equal the step instant (later is FutureDataError, earlier is OutOfOrderError); manifest and registry entries are exempt; a non-clock is a ConfigError; tick receive times never decrease across streams; a derived record is refused before any input; ClockReading advances the watermark; input_watermark_ns never moves back; reopening with a clock replays the history without the clock rule;
  - durability: written and fsynced before the record is visible, checked with an injected opener and a counting os.fsync; after a write or fsync error the journal is failed and refuses every later publish; open_for_restart then repairs the torn tail, with repaired_bytes reported;
  - commit() with fsync=False, and a failed commit;
  - the <path>.lock writer lock: a second writer is refused, also through open_for_restart, and the lock is released on close;
  - open_for_restart on an intact, a torn and a missing file, and still refusing every other kind of damage;
  - JournalReader: poll, read, streams and run_id while a writer is writing; a partly written last line is tolerated; the reader writes nothing and takes no lock; damaged lines are refused;
  - run_story() accepted in order, with and without a clock, and refused, with the journal unchanged, when a record that others depend on is left out.
- test_causality.py: OTHER_RUN_ID uses the new identity.run_id signature. New tests cover an OrderEvent as the derived record: accepted cases; refusal by each applicable rule (run, created before its input, outlives its input, origin); the watermark rule not applied to an order event; and the refusal of a child that is neither a produced record nor an order event.
- test_clock_reading.py: PAPER_RUN_ID now comes from the paper sample manifest. PollingScheduler is built on a PaperClock, and new tests check that it refuses anything else. A reading of a PaperClock shows the step instant, not the system clock, also through a ClockView.

Defects: none remain open. The one I found during the work was RegistryHistory.check and validate_registry_history raising a plain TypeError for a non-ModelVersion argument, which breaks the CLAUDE.md rule that errors derive from GieError. Another engineer fixed it in gie/contracts/model_version.py while I was working (it now raises ContractValidationError). The new test test_the_registry_refuses_what_is_not_a_model_version_with_a_gie_error failed before that change and passes after it.
